{"label":"1A","direction":"supports","claim":"In ENA human runs of at least 100 Gb (30x-genome scale), the NovaSeq X share of 6000+X releases rose from 0.5% to 11.8% to 18.6% in Q3'24, Q3'25 and Q3'26, so about 81% is still on the 6000.","detail":"I counted ENA human (tax 9606) read_runs with base_count ≥1e11, by first_public date, for Jul–Sep of each year. NovaSeq 6000 counts were 785 (2024), 771 (2025) and 610 (2026). NovaSeq X counts were 4, 103 and 139. X share = X/(6000+X): 4/789 = 0.5%, 103/874 = 11.8%, 139/749 = 18.6%. The X gained only 6.8 points in the latest year, against 11.3 points the year before. The 6000 still holds about 81% of WGS-scale public runs, which is consistent with a sticky 6000 tail in genome-scale work. Caveat: ENA is dominated by research and consortium batches, and first_public is the release date, not the sequencing date.","url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ena/portal/api/count?result=read_run&query=tax_eq(9606)%20AND%20instrument_model=%22Illumina%20NovaSeq%20X%22%20AND%20base_count%3E=100000000000%20AND%20first_public%3E=2026-07-01%20AND%20first_public%3C=2026-09-30","date":"2026-10-12","source_name":"EMBL-EBI ENA Portal API count endpoint","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"Adds a ≥100 Gb per-run bucket, which approximates a 30x genome. Earlier ENA work used ≥10 Gb filters and GEO series counts."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"supports","claim":"The X share of public human 10–100 Gb runs (exome/panel and low-pass scale) rose only from 12.2% to 18.1% in a year, and X volume was flat.","detail":"For ENA human runs with base_count from 1e10 to under 1e11, the 6000 had 14,884 runs in Q3'25 and 9,424 in Q3'26 (−36.7%). The X had 2,070 and 2,080 runs (+0.5%). X share = 2,070/16,954 = 12.2% in Q3'25 and 2,080/11,504 = 18.1% in Q3'26. The share gain therefore comes from 6000 runs being released less often, not from X growth. That is not the picture of a price-driven surge in X volume. A 5.9-point annual gain is consistent with an extra mix shift of roughly 5–6 points still to go.","url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ena/portal/api/count?result=read_run&query=tax_eq(9606)%20AND%20instrument_model=%22Illumina%20NovaSeq%20X%22%20AND%20base_count%3E=10000000000%20AND%20base_count%3C100000000000%20AND%20first_public%3E=2026-07-01%20AND%20first_public%3C=2026-09-30","date":"2026-10-12","source_name":"EMBL-EBI ENA Portal API","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"medium","why_new":"This is a mid-size run bucket with absolute counts for both platforms. Earlier work reported only share doubling at ≥10 Gb."},
{"label":"1C","direction":"supports","claim":"Public human WGS-scale (≥100 Gb) run releases on the 6000 plus X fell 14% y/y in Q3'26 (874 to 749) and mid-size runs fell 33%.","detail":"6000+X ≥100 Gb runs were 789 (Q3'24), 874 (Q3'25) and 749 (Q3'26). That is +10.8% in 2025 and −14.3% in 2026. For 10–100 Gb runs, 6000+X totalled 16,954 in Q3'25 and 11,504 in Q3'26 (−32.1%). Public human sequencing output on NovaSeq-family platforms is therefore shrinking in run count, not growing in the way a 30% Gb story would imply. Caveat: release timing is lumpy, many runs are not public, and run counts are not Gb.","url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ena/portal/api/count?result=read_run&query=tax_eq(9606)%20AND%20instrument_model=%22Illumina%20NovaSeq%206000%22%20AND%20base_count%3E=100000000000%20AND%20first_public%3E=2026-07-01%20AND%20first_public%3C=2026-09-30","date":"2026-10-12","source_name":"EMBL-EBI ENA Portal API","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"This is a Q3 y/y total of 6000+X runs at WGS scale. F700 covered a different public-run metric."},
{"label":"1A","direction":"against","claim":"Over Oct'25–Jun'26 the X share of ≥100 Gb human runs was only 8.5%, and 6000 releases stayed high, so ENA does not show a clean 6000 decline.","detail":"For the nine months Oct 2025–Jun 2026 there were 4,028 6000 runs and 373 X runs at ≥100 Gb. The X share is 373/4,401 = 8.5%. Those nine months already contain 6000 releases of about 1,340 per quarter, against 610 in Q3'26. The series is very lumpy, so the Q3'26 decline may reflect batch timing rather than a trend. This warns the analyst not to over-read single-quarter ENA shares.","url":"https://www.ebi.ac.uk/ena/portal/api/count?result=read_run&query=tax_eq(9606)%20AND%20instrument_model=%22Illumina%20NovaSeq%206000%22%20AND%20base_count%3E=100000000000%20AND%20first_public%3E=2025-10-01%20AND%20first_public%3C=2026-06-30","date":"2026-10-12","source_name":"EMBL-EBI ENA Portal API","source_type":"dataset","quote":"","strength":"weak","why_new":"Shows consortium batch volatility in the same series and rules out a simple monotonic read."},
